cd ~/k8s-journey/manifests/week5


STEP 1: OOMKill a container
---------------------------
vi pod-oom.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: oom
spec:
  containers:
  - name: stress
    image: polinux/stress
    command: ["stress"]
    args: ["--vm", "1", "--vm-bytes", "250M", "--vm-hang", "1"]
    resources:
      requests:
        memory: "50Mi"
      limits:
        memory: "100Mi"

k apply -f pod-oom.yaml
k get pod oom -w            # OOMKilled -> CrashLoopBackOff
k describe pod oom | grep -A6 "Last State"
k get pod oom -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated}{"\n"}'

Look for: reason OOMKilled, exitCode 137, restartCount growing.


STEP 2: Fix it
--------------
Change the limit to 300Mi (above the 250M the app uses):
  k delete pod oom
  sed -i 's/memory: "100Mi"/memory: "300Mi"/' pod-oom.yaml
  k apply -f pod-oom.yaml
  sleep 20 ; k top pod oom ; k get pod oom


STEP 3: Kernel evidence on the node
-----------------------------------
NODE=$(k get pod oom -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}')
docker exec $NODE dmesg 2>/dev/null | grep -i -E "oom|killed process" | tail -5
(dmesg may need privileges; also try on your host: sudo dmesg | grep -i oom | tail)
k delete pod oom


STEP 4: CPU throttling
----------------------
vi pod-cpu.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: cpu-limited
spec:
  containers:
  - name: burn
    image: polinux/stress
    command: ["stress"]
    args: ["--cpu", "2"]
    resources:
      requests:
        cpu: "100m"
      limits:
        cpu: "200m"

k apply -f pod-cpu.yaml
sleep 60
k top pod cpu-limited            # ~200m, never more

NODE=$(k get pod cpu-limited -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}')
CID=$(k get pod cpu-limited -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].containerID}' | cut -d/ -f3)
docker exec $NODE sh -c "find /sys/fs/cgroup -path '*$CID*' -name cpu.stat -exec cat {} \;"

nr_throttled and throttled_usec keep growing. The pod is alive and
never restarted, just slowed.

Remove the limit and compare k top (it now uses ~2 cores):
  k delete pod cpu-limited
  sed -i '/limits:/,/cpu: "200m"/d' pod-cpu.yaml
  k apply -f pod-cpu.yaml ; sleep 60 ; k top pod cpu-limited
  k delete pod cpu-limited


STEP 5: Measure latency impact of throttling
--------------------------------------------
k create deploy calc --image=python:3.12-alpine -- python -c "
import http.server, time
class H(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
    def do_GET(self):
        s=time.time(); sum(i*i for i in range(3_000_000))
        self.send_response(200); self.end_headers()
        self.wfile.write(f'{time.time()-s:.3f}s\n'.encode())
http.server.HTTPServer(('',8000),H).serve_forever()"
k expose deploy calc --port=8000
k run c --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 -- sh -c 'for i in 1 2 3; do wget -qO- calc:8000; done'

Now limit CPU to 100m and repeat:
  k set resources deploy calc --limits=cpu=100m
  k rollout status deploy calc
  k run c --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 -- sh -c 'for i in 1 2 3; do wget -qO- calc:8000; done'

The same work takes much longer. This is how CPU limits cause
"mysterious" slowness.
k delete deploy calc ; k delete svc calc


STEP 6: Node-pressure eviction (read and observe)
-------------------------------------------------
kind nodes share your machine's memory, so real eviction is hard to
trigger safely. Look at the configuration instead:
  docker exec lab-worker cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep -A5 -i evict
  k describe node lab-worker | grep -A8 Conditions

Optional (only with plenty of free RAM): run several BestEffort pods
with "stress --vm 1 --vm-bytes 1G --vm-hang 0" on one node (use
nodeName: lab-worker), watch "k get pods -w" and "k describe node
lab-worker" for MemoryPressure and Evicted pods. Delete them quickly.


